      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Feelings: The Brain's Color-Coded Sticky Notes",
      "keyInsight": "We assume our feelings are deep personal verdicts about reality. In reality, they are just quick three-way sorting tags. The moment any sight, sound, or thought arrives, your mind slaps a sticker on it: pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral. That is all feeling really is at the root level! The funny catch is that we mistake the sticker for a command. A pleasant tag makes us grab like a hungry shopper. An unpleasant tag makes us swat like a frantic hiker dodging a wasp. A neutral tag makes us tune out completely. If you spot the sticky note when it gets slapped on, the spell breaks immediately. You see the raw sensation before your automatic drama engine boots up. You do not have to fight the feeling or fix it. You just see the tag as a passing color, not a lifestyle order.",
      "content": "Imagine you run a bustling home kitchen. Every single ingredient that enters gets stamped by an assistant before you even look at it. Fresh strawberries get a bright green sticker. Burnt toast gets a red warning tag. Plain water gets a dull gray label. In the mind, this quick labeling system is what we call feeling.\n\nEvery sensory flash arrives with this instantaneous tone. It is not an elaborate thought yet. It is simply whether the contact tastes pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral. If you sit on a comfortable cushion, the pleasant tag goes up. If your knee begins to throb, the unpleasant tag flashes instantly. Most moments during a quiet walk feel completely neutral, like ordinary room temperature.\n\nHere is where everyday trouble starts for most of us. We do not just observe the tag. We instantly react to it like a fire alarm! We cling hard to the pleasant strawberry sticker, demanding five more bowls. We panic and fight the red throb tag, tensing our whole body against the ache. With neutral moments, our attention gets bored and drifts away to daydream about dinner.\n\nMindfulness steps into the kitchen as a calm inspector. You pause and notice the sticker being applied in real time. You see pleasantness as just pleasantness, and discomfort as plain discomfort. Neither tag defines you. When you watch the sticker arrive without grabbing or kicking, the automatic reaction circuit quietly powers down.",
